-- title:
--   Derivative form of Borel–Carathéodory with an intermediate radius
-- statement:
--   This is the Cauchy-estimate consequence of the Borel–Carathéodory inequality, stated with an explicit intermediate radius.
--
--   Let $f$ be analytic on the open disc $D(0,R)$ with $f(0) = 0$ and $\mathrm{Re}\, f \le M$ throughout, where $M > 0$. Let $0 < r < r' < R$. Then for every $z$ with $|z| \le r$,
--   $$|f'(z)| \;\le\; \frac{2M(r')^2}{(R - r')\,(r' - r)^2}.$$
--
--   The intermediate radius $r'$ mediates between the disc where the real-part bound lives and the disc where the derivative is estimated: Borel–Carathéodory bounds $|f|$ on $|z| \le r'$, and Cauchy's integral formula for the derivative converts that into a bound on $|f'|$ on the smaller disc $|z| \le r$. Keeping $r'$ free allows the constant to be optimised by the caller; choosing $r' = (R+r)/2$ gives the cleaner form stated separately.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/AlexKontorovich/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/blob/f55e85551ac10e96d98262a354cfcaac2825f2da/PrimeNumberTheoremAnd/StrongPNT.lean#L151-L178

